@startuml agent-flow
' Project Vestige agent flow. The rules behind it are in AGENTS.md, "How work is done" and "Lanes".
' Render: right-click in VS Code > Preview Current Diagram (PlantUML extension), or
' java -jar plantuml.jar -tpng .claude/agent-flow.puml
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Segoe UI
skinparam ActivityBorderColor #555555
skinparam ArrowColor #555555
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #FFF4E5
skinparam NoteBorderColor #C98A3A

title Project Vestige: from task to done

start
:**Task picked up**
Main session: roadmap item, the user's words;

:**Main session picks a lane**
Writes the lane and the tripwires it checked
in two lines at the top of the reviewer's brief;
note right
  **Tripwires** (any one means lane C):
  1. a new class, component or subsystem,
     or a change to an ownership row
  2. a new or changed data asset type or
     field, a content ID scheme, or anything saved
  3. a new engine framework or Build.cs
     dependency, or a new ability, effect
     or attribute
  4. per frame, timer or new-day work, or a
     loop over many objects
  5. a change to an interface other classes call,
     or to the layers or include direction
  6. a system with no guide in Docs/Systems/

  Unsure about a tripwire: lane C.
  Unsure about anything else: lane B.
  The code review checks the lane.
end note

#FFF1D6:**Game designer** (any lane, only when how the game plays
changes and GameDesign.md and the user's words don't settle it)
Prepares questions with options and a recommendation;
note right
  The user decides game feel
  and design; the designer
  records the answers
end note

fork
switch (Which lane?)
case (**Writing**\nlore, story, text with a voice)
  #F3E5F5:**Writer**
  Writes in Docs/Spoilers/, decides line-level
  writing and minor canon, asks only about big canon;
  note right
    No architect, no design review, no code review.
    Text for the game is marked Ready; the implementer
    enters it as FText data in lane A or B.
  end note
case (**Lane A**\ntrivial)
  repeat
    :**One agent** does the change
    Implementer for code, test author for tests,
    main session for docs;
  repeat while (**Code review** by the reviewer) is (changes needed)
  ->approved;
case (**Lane B**\ninside an established system)
  repeat
    #DCEBFA:**Implementer**
    Writes the C++, builds clean;
    #DDF3EE:**Test author**
    Tests (when a rule changes), full suite, PlayTest.md;
  repeat while (**Code review** by the reviewer
code, tests, docs) is (changes needed)
  ->approved;
case (**Lane C**\nnew design, a tripwire applies)
  repeat
    #E8E0F7:**Architect**
    Owner, layer, guide, test plan;
    note right
      Design questions go to the
      user through the designer.
      For the maintainer's yes:
      rule changes, licensing,
      money, anything that
      can't be undone
    end note
  repeat while (**Design review** by the reviewer) is (changes needed) not (approved)
  repeat
    #DCEBFA:**Implementer**
    Writes the C++, builds clean;
    #DDF3EE:**Test author**
    Tests, full suite, PlayTest.md;
  repeat while (**Code review** by the reviewer
code, tests, docs) is (changes needed)
  ->approved;
endswitch

fork again
  repeat
    #F9E1EC:**Artist** (when art is needed, in any lane)
    Blender script, export, import,
    preview renders;
  repeat while (**Art review** by the art reviewer
renders, mesh, scripts, licensing) is (changes needed)
  ->approved;

fork again
  repeat
    #E3F1F7:**Audio designer** (when sound is needed, in any lane)
    Audio plan, sound script or licensed
    recording, import, measurements;
    note right
      Can't hear: every new sound
      gets a listening check
      in PlayTest.md
    end note
  repeat while (**Sound review** by the reviewer
scripts, import, data, licensing) is (changes needed)
  ->approved;
end fork

:**Commit** (main session)
Build and suite verified after the last change, checklist;
#E2F2DF:**Done**
Report to the user lists each technical decision,
tagged easy or hard to undo;
stop

legend bottom left
  **Shortcuts**
  Bug fix: the test author writes a failing test first, then the implementer fixes it, then the code review.
  Working for a contributor: nothing stands in for the maintainer's review of the pull request.
  Art: the artist and the art review run alongside the code steps of any lane that needs art.
  Sound: the audio designer and its review run the same way; a new sound class or way of playing sounds goes to the architect.
  Editor and build steps never run at the same time, even across the code, art and sound branches.
  Typo fixes: no flow.
endlegend
@enduml
